 ## Overview
-Static Round-Robin Partitioning is a data distribution strategy for parallel volume rendering that partitions a volume into many small subvolumes and distributes them round-robin across processors to average out differences in projected area and element count [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from its parent [Round-Robin Data Distribution](2667-round-robin-data-distribution.md) by explicitly targeting load balancing through subvolume granularity rather than simple cell assignment, and from siblings [Scattered Assignment](4204-scattered-assignment.md) and [Randomized Data Decomposition](11592-randomized-data-decomposition.md) by relying on a fixed, pre-determined partition size rather than stochastic or spatially-aware assignment [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Static Round-Robin Partitioning is a load balancing strategy for distributed volume rendering that partitions a data volume into many small subvolumes and distributes them round-robin across processors to average out projected area and element count differences [S1, S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its siblings, [Scattered Assignment](4204-scattered-assignment.md) and [Randomized Data Decomposition](11592-randomized-data-decomposition.md), by using a deterministic, sequential assignment pattern rather than random or purely scattered mapping, though it shares the trait of destroying spatial locality [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike dynamic methods, it fixes the data-to-node mapping permanently after initial empirical tuning [S1, unofficial].
 
